+
+## Root causes of the gaps, found by looking at the sentences behind them (2026-11-24, real priors home)
+The stamped fact set, real home, whole-word judge; each row changes the hunt after reading the previous row's answers (REUSED, DEV-like: a new fact set is needed to know any of this generalises).
+| arm | right | wrong | gap |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| v3 (title match, own-page preference, lifespan, locative place, singular pronoun) | 5 | 3 | 23 |
+| v4 + every sentence of the subject's own page goes to the reader | 3 | 3 | 25 |
+| v5 + page-subject pronoun needs the subject's gender and the main clause; a date has a year; of places in a row the first answers | 8 | 3 | 20 |
+| v6 + no pronoun after another subject; a name inside another person's name is not the anchor; a capitalised relation word is a title word; a year is four digits or three with an era; a place named for the person is not his birthplace | 11 | 2 | 18 |
+Causes confirmed by experiment:
+1. The passage filter (the anchor's rarest word) dropped the sentences that state the facts on the subject's own page ("Dante was born in Florence." read alone gives Florence, but the word "alighieri" is not in it; "He was born in Frankfurt" has no name at all). Handing the whole page over alone made the result WORSE (5 to 3).
+2. Because the decision rule treats every candidate as a rival and declines when two disagree, FALSE CANDIDATES block correct answers, so more evidence meant more conflicts. The false candidates came from the hunt's own reading, each a precision defect: dates that are not dates ("two", "at the age of 79", "14", the page number 221); a pronoun taken for the page's subject when it is in a subordinate clause ("until he died in 1751"), has the wrong gender ("She died when Dante was ten") or follows a sentence about someone else ("Rudolf was his student. But he died in 1902"); a surname inside another person's name ("Christiane von Goethe died in 1816"); German title words read as the English verb ("Die falsche Spitzfindigkeit" is a verb "die" in a bibliography line); places said in a row ("in Königsberg in the Kingdom of Prussia": the first answers); a place named for the person ("Villa Wittgenstein"); two sentences run together by the extraction ("25 August.Concurring reports").
+3. All earlier arms ran on the stand-in priors home, where "Aristotle" and "Goethe" are not names.
+Still gaps (18): "Who wrote X?" (7): the author stands next to the work as a lead (Goethe, Wittgenstein, Kant are the leads) because authorship is said as a possessive ("Kant's Critique of Pure Reason"), which no tier reads; the article for the work or its author is not retrieved for 6 questions (Shakespeare birth, World War I, War and Peace, the Divine Comedy, Hamlet: common-word anchors prefer long documents); Nietzsche, Goethe, Wittgenstein birth place: the text says "Born on 15 October 1844, … grew up in Röcken", where the birth place is not under "born". Wrong (2): Wittgenstein's death "May 1902" (a brother, said with a bare "he"), Plato's teacher "Sophist" (an introduction by Jowett).
+
+## v7 to v11: wrong answers read, fixed, rerun (2026-11-24, real priors home, REUSED / DEV-like)
+The stamped fact set again (31 questions + 3 controls, whole-word judge, `KHORA_PRIORS_DIR=/Users/mlacy/Documents/janus/priors`, `EQUIV=1`). Every change after v6 was made knowing the previous answers, so none of this says anything about questions not yet seen: the FRESH set (questions-fresh.json, stamp 8c096587…) is still unread. Per-question time is not comparable across rows (v7 ran on cold caches).
+| run | right | wrong | gap | what changed |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| v7 | 15 | 3 | 13 | possessive authorship ("Kant's Prolegomena"); BM25 + a title index, pages named for the anchor first |
+| v8 | 17 | 0 | 14 | a title is the whole title: a capitalised word right before a name ("the Divine Comedy", "Thus Spoke Zarathustra") or a Roman numeral right after one ("World War I") joins the anchor; a piece the splitter cut off a sentence (verbless, or beginning in lower case) no longer clears whose sentence a bare "he" follows |
+| v9 | 17 | 1 | 13 | `&nbsp;` in a passage is a space; two sentences run together by the extraction ("on 25 August.Concurring reports") are split; a day and a month with no year take the year the sentence gave before them; a word hung from a full stop is re-hung from the verb before it; the name-folding rule ("Shakespeare" into "William Shakespeare") no longer folds a year into a date |
+| v10 | 18 | 0 | 13 | a link's target is cut from its display text ("Stagira (ancient city)Stagira" is "Stagira"); "the city of Stagira in Chalkidiki": the locative is on the settlement noun, so the city answers and the region is its container (v9 had said "Chalkidiki", wrong) |
+| v11 | 18 | 0 | 13 | the repeated link text in a fused name is cut ("Dante AlighieriDante" is "Dante Alighieri"); controls 3 gaps; the same code as the hunt tests |
+Hand check of the 18 right answers against the sentence each came from (the stamped rule only needs the accepted word, so a right year with a wrong day would have passed): all 18 are right as said, including the days (Kant 22 April 1724 and 12 February 1804, Dante died "September 14 1321", Nietzsche "25 August 1900", Tolstoy "20 November 1910"). Two of v8's "right" were not: Nietzsche "30 August 1900" (the funeral date, later in the same fused sentence) and Wittgenstein "25 April 1951" (a manuscript begun that day, promoted by a year-only lifespan from another page); both are fixed in v9 to v11 (Nietzsche "25 August 1900"; Wittgenstein "1951", the year, because no sentence on his page says the day). "Professor Kant" keeps its title.
+Root causes found by reading the sentence behind each wrong answer:
+1. The splitter cut "… crying "Wrong! Wrong!" when two brass bands …" in two, so the guard "a bare he right after a sentence about someone else is theirs" saw a fragment with no named subject and let a brother's death (May 1902) become Wittgenstein's.
+2. The anchor lost its title words ("Divine"), so "Who wrote the Divine Comedy?" read the Dante page for "Comedy" and answered "Latin".
+3. The reader makes the numeral of "World War I" a pronoun, so the page "World War II" answered "1 September 1939".
+4. A rule written for names ("the shorter folds into the longer that ends in it") applied to dates: the year 1951 from a lifespan folded into "25 April 1951", which another sentence says of a manuscript.
+5. Under the real parser, "did" is the root verb of "Who did Mel Gibson play …?" (the relation came out "do"), a participle ("directed, written …") hangs from its noun by the unspecified label, and a sentence-final phrase hangs from the full stop. Each cost an older test its answer under the real home; all three older tests (the void of a question, the cast-list lead, the participle chain) now pass under both homes.
+Still gaps (13 of 31): birth place or date said as a noun or in a parenthesis the extraction broke (Nietzsche: "(15 October 1844 – 25 August 1900)" sits behind a pronunciation template's residue; Goethe, Wittgenstein "birthplace"; Aristotle, Shakespeare, Nietzsche birth dates); who wrote the Tractatus, Zarathustra, Hamlet (the author stands next to the work as a lead, no sentence says "wrote"); Plato's and Aristotle's teacher and the Byzantine capital (a noun relation: the hunt's noun tier reads the copula, not "taught by"); World War I begin (the page says "outbreak", "broke out": a nominal for a verb, the same defect as "birthplace"); controls (3, correctly).
+Tests: hunt, source and conversation suites, 48 pass and 1 skipped under the real home, 47 pass and 2 skipped under the stand-in (the two skips are a real-home-only test and the live Wikipedia smoke test).
